  "articleBody": "It's super frustrating when you've put in the effort to patch drywall, but it just sticks out like a sore thumb, right? One of the biggest reasons for a patch not blending is applying the joint compound too thickly. Those thick layers don't dry evenly and can create bumps or ridges. Think thin coats – it's always better to apply a few thin layers than one big glob. Another common mistake is not feathering the edges far enough. You need to spread that compound out a bit, gradually tapering it into the existing wall, so there's no harsh line. And speaking of drying, are you sanding before it's completely dry? That's a big no-no! Depending on humidity and coat thickness, it can take hours or even overnight for each coat to dry. Sanding too early will just mess up your work. Also, if you’re using the wrong type of patch material for the damage size, it might not give you the smooth finish you're hoping for. Sometimes, the underlying problem—like a settling house or recurring moisture—is causing the damage. If you've tried everything and that patch still isn't blending, or if you suspect there's a bigger issue, don't hesitate to give a pro like HH Remodeling LLC a call. We can make sure your walls look flawless.",
